#{[17592186085088 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085093] [17592186085468 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085469] [17592186085473 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085474] [17592186085478 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085480] [17592186085483 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085485] [17592186085489 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085493] [17592186085494 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085496] [17592186085499 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085501] [17592186085504 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186085506] [17592186086586 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186086588] [17592186086604 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186086608] [17592186087143 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087148] [17592186087149 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087153] [17592186087179 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087184] [17592186087269 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087273] [17592186087317 :answer-field/correct #edb/ref 17592186087319] [17592186087458 :question/worked-solution "A first-order ordinary differential equation that is written in the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}\n    =\n    f(x, y)\n$$\n\nis said to be homogeneous if $f(x,y)$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero, meaning that it satisfies\n\n$$\nf(tx, ty) = f(x, y).\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's take a look at the right-hand side function in each differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x}$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot x} \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{x} \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation I is a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{x^2}$ is *not* a homogeneous function of degree $0.$ Rather, it is a homogeneous function of degree $-1\\mathbin{:}$\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{(ty)}{(tx)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{{t}^{2}\\cdot {x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot \\frac{y}{{x}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t}\\cdot f(x,y) \\\\  & ={t}^{-1}f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{red}\\times }\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation II is *not* a homogenous differential equation.\n\n- The function $$\n\\displaystyle f(x,y) = \\dfrac{y}{2x}\\sin{\\left(\\dfrac{x^2}{y^2}\\right)}\n$$ is a homogeneous function of degree zero:\n \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(tx,ty) & =\\frac{ty}{2(tx)}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{(tx)^{2}}{(ty)^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\frac{t\\cdot y}{t\\cdot 2x}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{{t}^{2}{x}^{2}}{{t}^{2}{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =\\frac{y}{2x}\\operatorname{sin}\\left(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{y}^{2}}\\right) \\\\  & =f(x,y)\\hspace{1em}{\\color{green}✓}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, equation III is a homogenous differential equation.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\""] [17592186088017 :question/worked-solution "The decay model has solution $N(t)=230000e^{-kt}$. Use the measurement after five years:\n\n$$\n23000=230000e^{-5k}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad k=\\frac{\\ln10}{5}\\approx0.460517.\n$$\n\nThe continuous decay rate is approximately $46\\%$ per year, to the nearest percent."] [17592186088059 :question/worked-solution "The equation is in standard form with $P(x)=1$ and $Q(x)=\\cos x$. An integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int 1\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^x.\n$$\nMultiplying through by $e^x$ and using the product rule gives\n$$\ne^xy'+e^xy=e^x\\cos x,\\qquad \\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}x}(ye^x)=e^x\\cos x.\n$$"] [17592186088140 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+\\frac{2}{x}y=3,\\hspace{2em}x>0.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below:\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088140 :question/worked-solution "For $x>0$, an integrating factor is\n$$\nI(x)=e^{\\int(2/x)\\,\\mathrm{d}x}=e^{2\\ln x}=x^2.\n$$\nMultiplying by $x^2$ and integrating gives\n$$\n(x^2y)'=3x^2,\\qquad x^2y=x^3+C,\\qquad y=x+\\frac{C}{x^2}.\n$$\nDifferentiation gives $y'+2y/x=3$, as required."] [17592186088141 :question/problem "Consider the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=1,\\hspace{2em}y(0)=-3\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The solution to the initial value problem is $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088141 :question/worked-solution "An integrating factor is $I(x)=e^x$. Thus\n$$\n(e^xy)'=e^x,\\qquad e^xy=e^x+C,\\qquad y=1+Ce^{-x}.\n$$\nThe initial condition gives $-3=1+C$, so $C=-4$. Therefore\n$$\ny(x)=1-4e^{-x}=1-\\frac4{e^x}.\n$$\nThis satisfies both $y'+y=1$ and $y(0)=-3$."] [17592186088142 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=3x.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088142 :question/worked-solution "First divide by $2$:\n$$\ny'+\\frac12y=\\frac32x.\n$$\nAn integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{x/2}$. Integration by parts gives\n$$\n(ye^{x/2})'=\\frac32xe^{x/2},\\qquad ye^{x/2}=3(x-2)e^{x/2}+C.\n$$\nHence\n$$\ny=3(x-2)+Ce^{-x/2}.\n$$\nSubstitution verifies $2y'+y=3x$."] [17592186088143 :question/problem "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}-2y={x}^{2}{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n$\\hspace{1em}$ An integrating factor for this equation is $I(x)=$ {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\hspace{1em}$ The general solution is given by $y(x)=$ {{field-2}}."] [17592186088143 :question/worked-solution "An integrating factor is $I(x)=e^{-2x}$. Multiplying and integrating gives\n$$\n(ye^{-2x})'=x^2,\\qquad ye^{-2x}=\\frac{x^3}{3}+C.\n$$\nTherefore\n$$\ny=\\frac{x^3e^{2x}}3+Ce^{2x}.\n$$\nDifferentiating verifies $y'-2y=x^2e^{2x}$."] [17592186088322 :question/worked-solution "Compare with $\\frac{x^2}{a^2}+\\frac{y^2}{b^2}=1$. Here $a=\\sqrt{10}$ and $b=3$, with $a>b$. The lengths of the major and minor axes are therefore\n\n$$\n2a=2\\sqrt{10},\\qquad2b=6.\n$$"] [17592186088325 :question/worked-solution "The graph crosses the $x$-axis at $\\pm3$ and the $y$-axis at $\\pm4$. Thus the horizontal and vertical semiaxes are $3$ and $4$, respectively. Its equation is\n\n$$\n\\frac{x^2}{3^2}+\\frac{y^2}{4^2}=1,\\qquad\\text{or}\\qquad\\frac{x^2}{9}+\\frac{y^2}{16}=1.\n$$"] [17592186088415 :question/worked-solution "The initial depth is $D(0)=300$ feet. Solving $D^{\\prime}=0.01D$ gives\n\n$$\nD(t)=300e^{0.01t}.\n$$\n\nAfter six years,\n\n$$\nD(6)=300e^{0.06}\\approx318.550964\\text{ feet}.\n$$\n\nRounded to the nearest integer, the depth is $319$ feet."]}
